M="biochem-metab-1"
SUB="Metabolism"
T={
"sites":[("Which pathway occurs in BOTH mitochondria and cytoplasm?",["Urea cycle","Glycolysis","Fatty acid β-oxidation","HMP shunt"],0,"Heme synthesis, the urea cycle and gluconeogenesis all cross both compartments."),
 ("Why can a mature red blood cell not perform β-oxidation?",["It has no mitochondria","It lacks carnitine","Its membrane excludes fatty acids","It lacks NAD+"],0,"Without mitochondria the RBC depends entirely on glycolysis and can only make lactate from pyruvate."),
 ("A patient has a defect in the first and last enzymes of heme synthesis but normal intermediate steps. Where do those two enzymes act?",["Mitochondria","Cytosol","Peroxisome","Smooth endoplasmic reticulum"],0,"Heme synthesis starts and ends in mitochondria with the middle steps in cytosol, which is why defects can appear at either compartment.",2)],
"ratelim":[("The rate-determining enzyme of glycolysis is:",["Phosphofructokinase-1","Hexokinase","Pyruvate kinase","Glucose-6-phosphatase"],0,"PFK-1, not hexokinase, is the committed and rate-limiting step."),
 ("Fructose-2,6-bisphosphate has which combined effect?",["Activates PFK-1 and inhibits fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase","Activates both enzymes equally","Inhibits PFK-1 and activates fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase","Has no effect on either enzyme"],0,"One molecule flips both directions at once, so glycolysis and gluconeogenesis are never fast together."),
 ("A patient on a high-carbohydrate meal has high insulin. Which change best explains the fall in fatty acid oxidation?",["Malonyl-CoA inhibits carnitine acyltransferase I","Acetyl-CoA carboxylase is inhibited by citrate","HMG-CoA synthase is activated","Glycogen phosphorylase is activated"],0,"Insulin activates acetyl-CoA carboxylase, raising malonyl-CoA, which blocks fatty acid entry into mitochondria so synthesis and oxidation do not run together.",3)],
"carriers":[("NADPH is used mainly for:",["Reductive synthesis and antioxidant defence","Electron transport in the respiratory chain","Transfer of acyl groups between enzymes","Carboxylation using bicarbonate"],0,"NADH feeds the respiratory chain; NADPH drives biosynthesis and glutathione regeneration."),
 ("Which enzyme requires biotin?",["Pyruvate carboxylase","Pyruvate dehydrogenase","Transketolase","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase"],0,"Also acetyl-CoA carboxylase and propionyl-CoA carboxylase. Biotin carries CO2."),
 ("A malnourished man develops confusion, ophthalmoplegia and ataxia. Which group of enzymes is impaired?",["Those requiring thiamine pyrophosphate","Those requiring biotin","Those requiring S-adenosylmethionine","Those requiring tetrahydrofolate"],0,"Thiamine deficiency impairs pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, branched-chain ketoacid dehydrogenase and transketolase.",2)],
"glyco":[("The net yield of anaerobic glycolysis per glucose is:",["2 ATP and 2 NADH","4 ATP and 2 NADH","2 ATP and 4 NADH","6 ATP and no NADH"],0,"Four ATP are made but two are invested in the early steps."),
 ("Which glycolytic step is irreversible?",["Phosphofructokinase-1","Phosphoglycerate kinase","Phosphoglycerate mutase","Triose phosphate isomerase"],0,"Hexokinase/glucokinase, PFK-1 and pyruvate kinase are the three irreversible steps that gluconeogenesis must bypass."),
 ("Arsenic poisoning allows glycolysis to continue but yields no ATP from the glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate step. The reason is that arsenate:",["Substitutes for phosphate, giving an unstable product","Irreversibly inhibits the dehydrogenase enzyme","Blocks regeneration of NAD+ from NADH","Prevents the aldolase cleavage reaction"],0,"Arsenate replaces inorganic phosphate, so the high-energy acyl-phosphate never forms and the energy is lost as heat.",3)],
"hkgk":[("Compared with hexokinase, glucokinase has:",["A higher Km and higher Vmax","A lower Km and lower Vmax","A higher Km and lower Vmax","The same kinetics but different tissue distribution"],0,"Low affinity, high capacity: the liver takes up glucose only when blood glucose is high."),
 ("Glucokinase is induced by:",["Insulin","Glucagon","Epinephrine","Cortisol"],0,"It is the liver and beta-cell isoenzyme, raised after a meal."),
 ("A 19-year-old with mild, stable fasting hyperglycaemia since childhood has a strong autosomal dominant family history and no autoantibodies. A defect in which protein best explains this?",["Glucokinase","Hexokinase","GLUT4","Insulin receptor"],0,"Glucokinase is the beta-cell glucose sensor; loss-of-function raises the threshold for insulin release, giving MODY rather than type 1 or type 2 diabetes.",3)],
"glycoreg":[("Citrate inhibits PFK-1 because it signals that:",["Energy and building blocks are plentiful","Glucose supply has become scarce","The cell has turned hypoxic","NAD+ urgently needs regenerating"],0,"Citrate spilling out of a full TCA cycle slows glycolysis and promotes fatty acid synthesis."),
 ("Glucagon raises cAMP and activates protein kinase A in hepatocytes. The effect on the bifunctional PFK-2/FBPase-2 enzyme is to:",["Phosphorylate it, lowering fructose-2,6-bisphosphate","Phosphorylate it, raising fructose-2,6-bisphosphate","Dephosphorylate it, raising fructose-2,6-bisphosphate","Degrade the enzyme"],0,"Phosphorylation switches off the kinase domain and switches on the phosphatase domain, so gluconeogenesis prevails."),
 ("Which allosteric signal activates PFK-1 during vigorous exercise?",["AMP","ATP","Citrate","NADH"],0,"A falling energy charge raises AMP, which accelerates glycolysis.",2)],
"pdh":[("The pyruvate dehydrogenase complex requires all of the following EXCEPT:",["Biotin","Thiamine pyrophosphate","Lipoic acid","FAD"],0,"Its five cofactors are TPP, lipoic acid, CoA, FAD and NAD+. Biotin belongs to the carboxylases."),
 ("Arsenic inhibits lipoic acid. Which other enzyme is therefore also impaired?",["Alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase","Pyruvate carboxylase","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase","Glycogen phosphorylase"],0,"It uses the identical set of five cofactors, so the two fail together."),
 ("A neonate has severe lactic acidosis, poor feeding and neurological impairment; serum alanine and pyruvate are both raised. Which dietary change is most appropriate?",["Increase fat and ketogenic amino acids","Increase the dietary carbohydrate load","Restrict protein intake completely","Give high-dose biotin supplements"],0,"In pyruvate dehydrogenase deficiency, pyruvate cannot enter the TCA cycle. A ketogenic diet supplies acetyl-CoA downstream of the block; more carbohydrate would worsen the lactate.",3)],
"pyrfate":[("In the Cori cycle, what travels from muscle to liver?",["Lactate","Alanine","Glucose","Glycerol"],0,"The liver reconverts it to glucose, which returns to muscle."),
 ("The Cahill (glucose-alanine) cycle carries which additional substance to the liver?",["Amino nitrogen","Free fatty acids","Ketone bodies","Ammonia as urea"],0,"Alanine delivers both a carbon skeleton for gluconeogenesis and nitrogen for the urea cycle."),
 ("Pyruvate carboxylase is activated by acetyl-CoA. Physiologically this means that when acetyl-CoA accumulates, pyruvate is directed towards:",["Oxaloacetate for gluconeogenesis","Lactate for the Cori cycle","Acetyl-CoA for the TCA cycle","Alanine for nitrogen transport"],0,"A high acetyl-CoA signals that energy needs are met, so carbon is diverted to glucose synthesis instead of being burned.",3)],
"tca":[("Per acetyl-CoA, one turn of the TCA cycle produces:",["3 NADH, 1 FADH2, 1 GTP","2 NADH, 2 FADH2, 1 GTP","4 NADH, 1 FADH2, 2 GTP","3 NADH, 2 FADH2, no GTP"],0,"Plus two molecules of CO2."),
 ("Which enzyme belongs to both the TCA cycle and the electron transport chain?",["Succinate dehydrogenase","Citrate synthase","Aconitase","Malate dehydrogenase"],0,"It is complex II and the only TCA enzyme embedded in the inner mitochondrial membrane."),
 ("TCA intermediates are constantly withdrawn for heme and amino acid synthesis. Which reaction best replenishes them?",["Pyruvate carboxylase forming oxaloacetate","Citrate synthase forming citrate","Isocitrate dehydrogenase forming alpha-ketoglutarate","Succinate dehydrogenase forming fumarate"],0,"Anaplerotic reactions refill the cycle; pyruvate carboxylase is the main one. The others move carbon within the cycle rather than adding to it.",3)],
"etc":[("NADH yields more ATP than FADH2 because it:",["Enters before an extra proton-pumping site","Carries two more electrons per molecule","Is oxidised faster by complex III","Binds directly to the ATP synthase"],0,"FADH2 enters via complex II and bypasses the first pump."),
 ("Which complex contains copper and reduces oxygen to water?",["Complex IV","Complex I","Complex II","Complex III"],0,"Cytochrome oxidase. Its copper centres are required for the four-electron reduction of O2."),
 ("Energy from electron flow is used immediately to:",["Pump protons across the inner membrane","Phosphorylate ADP directly at each complex","Reduce NAD+ back to NADH","Transport pyruvate into the matrix"],0,"The proton gradient is the intermediate; ATP synthase then converts it into ATP.",2)],
"etcpois":[("Cyanide and carbon monoxide both act at:",["Complex IV","Complex I","Complex III","ATP synthase"],0,"They block cytochrome oxidase, halting electron flow and ATP synthesis together."),
 ("How does 2,4-dinitrophenol differ from oligomycin?",["It dissipates the gradient, speeding electron flow","It stops electron flow through the chain completely","It blocks complex I selectively and reversibly","It inhibits the ATP/ADP translocase instead"],0,"DNP is a lipophilic proton carrier, so oxygen consumption rises but the energy is released as heat rather than ATP."),
 ("A man taking an illicit weight-loss drug presents with temperature 41 C, drenching sweat, tachycardia and a rising lactate; his oxygen consumption is high. Which effect explains his presentation?",["Uncoupling of oxidative phosphorylation","Inhibition of cytochrome oxidase","Blockade of ATP synthase alone","Inhibition of pyruvate dehydrogenase"],0,"Uncouplers such as 2,4-dinitrophenol let protons leak back, so substrate is burned fast and the energy appears as heat. An inhibitor would lower oxygen consumption instead.",3)],
}

BOSS=dict(th="เคสใหญ่: ชายหนุ่มซึมหลังดื่มหนักและอดอาหาร",
 en="Boss case: Lactic acidosis after a binge and a fast",
 case="A 24-year-old man is brought in confused after three days of heavy drinking with almost no food. He is tachypnoeic. Glucose 48 mg/dL, lactate 7.2 mmol/L, anion gap 22. He improves after thiamine, then glucose.",
 qs=[
 ("Thiamine is given before glucose because thiamine pyrophosphate is required by:",["Pyruvate and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase","Pyruvate carboxylase and acetyl-CoA carboxylase","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase in the HMP shunt","Glycogen phosphorylase in liver and muscle"],0,"Giving glucose first drives pyruvate into a blocked complex, worsening lactic acidosis and risking Wernicke encephalopathy."),
 ("His lactate is high mainly because pyruvate cannot:",["Be converted to acetyl-CoA","Be converted to alanine","Leave the cytosol","Be formed from glucose"],0,"With pyruvate dehydrogenase impaired, pyruvate is shunted to lactate to regenerate NAD+."),
 ("Ethanol metabolism raises the NADH/NAD+ ratio. This worsens hypoglycaemia by inhibiting:",["Gluconeogenesis","Glycolysis","The HMP shunt","Ketogenesis"],0,"A high NADH/NAD+ ratio pushes oxaloacetate to malate and pyruvate to lactate, removing gluconeogenic substrate."),
 ("Which enzyme is the rate-determining step of the pathway that his liver cannot run?",["Fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase 1","Phosphofructokinase-1","Isocitrate dehydrogenase","HMG-CoA reductase"],0,"Gluconeogenesis is controlled at fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase 1."),
 ("His glycogen stores are exhausted after three days. Which organ's glycogen could not have supported his blood glucose even when full?",["Skeletal muscle","Liver","Both equally","Neither stores glycogen"],0,"Muscle lacks glucose-6-phosphatase, so its glycogen serves only the muscle itself."),
 ("After recovery, which single deficiency would most closely reproduce his original biochemical picture?",["Thiamine (B1)","Biotin (B7)","Niacin (B3)","Folate (B9)"],0,"Thiamine deficiency impairs the same TPP-dependent dehydrogenases that caused his lactate to rise."),
 ],
 pass_=5)
